import obsidian("artifacts/catln-test/Properties/LinksInTheWriting.md")
import obsidian("artifacts/catln-test/Properties/LinksOnlyInProperties.md")


# Tests importing an Obsidian vault. Every note of the vault becomes a page of its own, at any
  depth, and the vault's own .obsidian configuration is not read as notes.
  The notes are named here as well as the vault, and that is what gives them goldens. A vault does
  not import its notes — it holds them, and each says for itself what it links to — so the pages of
  a vault are not reachable from the page the vault is, and a golden test walking out from this file
  reaches the vault and stops. The notes named here reach the rest of the vault by linking, so what
  every note of it is read as is written down and compared rather than left to be taken on trust.
  The notes beyond the first two are the shapes a note takes, one each: how a note breaks at its
  checkboxes and its fences (@Shapes.md@, @FenceOpen.md@), which blocks are queries (@Queries.md@,
  @QueryUnclosed.md@), how wiki links are written as links and which note each names (@Links.md@,
  @Resolve.md@), and what the properties a note opens with name (@Properties/@). The goldens of
  each are what hold the reading; the convert golden shows the imports a note's links became and the
  writing its comments carry, which the desugared one does not.
# A page written rather than read, which is compiled and typechecked here and NOT displayed.
  It was written to hold the display of a note's page down: the annotations and values of a page are
  evaluated for the file a test names and not for the pages it reaches, so the pages of the vault
  above are read, desugared, typechecked and built while their values are never asked for.
  It does not hold it down, because a value declared in a @.ct@ page is not displayed at all. Such a
  declaration reaches the typechecked program carrying an arrow, which @isValueObjArr@ reads as a
  definition rather than a value, so the display of it is never asked for; the same declaration built
  by the Obsidian reader carries none and is displayed. Nothing is reported when that happens —
  @evalDShowAnnot@ answers an unreachable display with no value and no note — so a test naming this
  file passes either way. The same is true of the values in @valueDeclFields.ct@.
  What still holds is that the type and the arm displaying it compile and typecheck here. When a
  @.ct@ declaration is displayed like a read one, this becomes the gate it was written to be.
/Data/Note/Page(title= "A page written rather than read")

main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
